export const home: string = "<h2>Tasbih Counter Online — free digital tasbeeh and dhikr counter</h2>\n<p>A calm online tasbih counter for everyday dhikr. Tap anywhere to count, set a target of 33, 99, 100 or any number you like, and watch the bead ring fill as you go. It works as a digital tasbih, a tasbeeh counter, a zikr counter and a digital misbaha, it saves your count on your own device, and it keeps counting with no internet at all. There is nothing to install and no account to create.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is\">What is a tasbih counter?</h2>\n<p>A tasbih counter is a tool for keeping count while you repeat a remembrance of Allah. The traditional form is a string of prayer beads, called a misbaha or a tasbeeh, usually with thirty-three or ninety-nine beads. A digital tasbih counter does the same job on a screen: you tap once for each repetition and the number rises, so your attention can stay on the words rather than on the arithmetic.</p>\n<p>This page is an online tasbih counter you can use straight away. Some people search for it as a tasbeeh counter, some as a dhikr counter or a zikr counter, and in Turkey the same tool is usually called a zikirmatik. In Indonesia it is a tasbih digital or a penghitung tasbih, in France a compteur tasbih. They all describe one thing: a counter that keeps your place while you remember Allah.</p>\n<p>You may also know it as an electronic tasbih, an e-tasbih, a tasbih clicker, a tally counter for dhikr, a finger tasbih or a virtual tasbeeh. On the web it is found by searches such as tasbih online, tasbeeh online, online zikr counter, digital tasbih counter and free tasbih counter for mobile. All of them lead to the same free tool on this page.</p>\n<p>Counting on the fingers is the way taught in the sunnah, and a counter is a convenience, not a replacement for it. Use whichever helps you stay consistent.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-use\">How to use this online tasbeeh counter</h2>\n<p>The counter is the whole area around the number. You do not have to aim at a small button, which is the single most common complaint about digital counters. Tap anywhere on that surface and the count moves by one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Choose your dhikr:</strong> Pick SubhanAllah, Alhamdulillah, Allahu Akbar, La ilaha illallah, Astaghfirullah or Salawat from the Library beside the counter, or tap the dhikr bar on a phone and search the full list, the 99 Names, Tawaf and Sa'i.</li>\n<li><strong>Set a target:</strong> Tap Target and choose 33, 99, 100, 313, 1000 or type any number of your own, up to seven digits. Turn on rounds to split a long count into cycles of 11, 33 or 100.</li>\n<li><strong>Tap to count:</strong> Tap the counter, press Space or Enter on a keyboard, press Z or Backspace to undo the last count, and press F for full screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Watch the ring:</strong> The bead ring fills as you approach the target and the warm marker bead travels with your progress.</li>\n<li><strong>Finish or continue:</strong> When the target completes you can carry on counting, start another round, or finish the session. Nothing is destroyed for you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"features\">Features of this free digital tasbih counter</h2>\n<p>Everything below is free. There is no trial, no locked core feature and no account wall in front of the counter.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tap anywhere:</strong> A large tap surface built for one thumb, kept clear of the phone's own gesture bar so you never trigger the back gesture by mistake.</li>\n<li><strong>Undo and reset:</strong> A fifty-step undo, and a reset that always asks before it clears anything.</li>\n<li><strong>Targets and rounds:</strong> A total goal, an optional round size, and a round counter that stays off until you ask for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Countdown mode:</strong> Count down to zero instead of up, if that suits you better.</li>\n<li><strong>Timed dhikr:</strong> Timed sittings from 5 up to 60 minutes with the count running alongside.</li>\n<li><strong>Auto count:</strong> Let the counter count for you at a steady pace from one to three seconds a count, so your hands stay free. It stops by itself when your target completes.</li>\n<li><strong>Sound and vibration:</strong> A quiet tick and a haptic strong enough to feel with your eyes closed. Both are optional.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep screen awake:</strong> The screen stays on while you count, where the browser supports it.</li>\n<li><strong>Works offline:</strong> After the first visit the counter opens and counts with no connection at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Installable:</strong> Add it to your home screen and it opens like an app, on Android, iPhone, iPad, tablet and desktop.</li>\n<li><strong>Full screen and themes:</strong> A distraction-free full-screen counter, and Light, Dark, Midnight Noor and Emerald Heritage themes, or follow your system. All free.</li>\n<li><strong>Nine languages:</strong> English, Arabic, Urdu, Indonesian, Hindi, Turkish, Bengali, Malay and French for numbers and dates, with Arabic or Eastern Arabic numerals.</li>\n<li><strong>Your own dhikr:</strong> Create unlimited custom dhikr with your own Arabic, meaning and default target. It stays private on your device.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"targets-rounds\">Targets, rounds and what the numbers mean</h2>\n<p>A target is the number you are working towards in this sitting. A round is a cycle inside that target: with a round size of one hundred and a goal of one thousand you will complete ten rounds. The two are separate on purpose, and rounds never switch themselves on just because the target is large.</p>\n<p>Two kinds of target appear in this app and the difference is deliberate. A target marked Source available has reviewed evidence behind it, such as the thirty-three, thirty-three and thirty-four counted after the obligatory prayer. A target marked Your goal is simply a number you have chosen. Numbers like 313 or 1000 are offered as personal goals and nothing more.</p>\n<p>This app will never tell you that a particular repetition count produces a particular result. If you want to count 4444 or 125,000 of something, type it in and the counter will track it faithfully, without attaching a claim to it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stats\">Today, streak and lifetime totals</h2>\n<p>Three numbers sit above the counter. Today is everything you have counted since your own local midnight. Rounds is how many rounds you have completed, and Total is your lifetime count. Your streak, the run of consecutive days on which you completed at least one round — a target you set, or thirty-three counts — is shown beside the counter under Your practice and on the Streak page.</p>\n<p>Days are worked out from your device's own calendar, not from a server clock, so travelling across time zones will not break a streak and a day never disappears later. The tone here stays calm on purpose: there are no guilt messages, no lost-streak warnings and no leaderboards. Continue whenever you are ready.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dhikr-list\">Dhikr you can count here</h2>\n<p>Each entry carries its Arabic, a transliteration and a plain meaning. The Arabic is set in a proper Arabic face at a readable size, and long phrases wrap onto as many lines as they need instead of being cut off.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SubhanAllah counter:</strong> سُبْحَانَ ٱللَّٰهِ — Glory be to Allah. Commonly counted thirty-three times after salah, or one hundred times as a daily tasbih.</li>\n<li><strong>Alhamdulillah counter:</strong> ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّٰهِ — All praise is for Allah. The second part of the after-salah tasbih.</li>\n<li><strong>Allahu Akbar counter:</strong> ٱللَّٰهُ أَكْبَرُ — Allah is the greatest. Counted thirty-four times to complete the hundred, or thirty-three in the variant that closes with the tahlil.</li>\n<li><strong>Astaghfirullah counter:</strong> أَسْتَغْفِرُ ٱللَّٰهَ — I seek forgiveness from Allah. Often kept as a daily istighfar counter of one hundred.</li>\n<li><strong>La ilaha illallah counter:</strong> لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا ٱللَّٰهُ — There is no god but Allah. Also searched as a tahlil counter or a kalima counter.</li>\n<li><strong>Durood and salawat counter:</strong> ٱللَّٰهُمَّ صَلِّ عَلَىٰ مُحَمَّدٍ — blessings upon the Prophet. Spelled durood, darood or darud, and often counted on Friday.</li>\n<li><strong>SubhanAllahi wa bihamdihi:</strong> سُبْحَانَ ٱللَّٰهِ وَبِحَمْدِهِ — narrated as a remembrance said one hundred times in a day.</li>\n<li><strong>La hawla wa la quwwata illa billah:</strong> لَا حَوْلَ وَلَا قُوَّةَ إِلَّا بِٱللَّٰهِ — there is no power except by Allah.</li>\n<li><strong>Hasbunallahu wa ni'mal wakeel:</strong> حَسْبُنَا ٱللَّٰهُ وَنِعْمَ ٱلْوَكِيلُ — Allah is sufficient for us.</li>\n<li><strong>Dua Yunus:</strong> لَا إِلَٰهَ إِلَّا أَنْتَ سُبْحَانَكَ إِنِّي كُنْتُ مِنَ ٱلظَّالِمِينَ — from Surah al-Anbiya, also known as Ayat e Karima.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"after-salah\">The 33-33-34 dhikr after salah</h2>\n<p>The remembrance after the obligatory prayer is the most searched routine in this whole category, and it is the one thing most counters handle badly. Here it is a guided routine: choose it once and the counter moves to the next phrase by itself when each part completes.</p>\n<p>Two forms are offered because two are transmitted. The first counts SubhanAllah thirty-three times, Alhamdulillah thirty-three times and Allahu Akbar thirty-four times, reaching one hundred. The second counts each of the three thirty-three times and completes the hundred with the tahlil. Follow the way you have been taught. Neither is presented here as the only correct method.</p>\n<p>Tasbih Fatimah uses the same thirty-three, thirty-three and thirty-four structure and is offered separately, because the occasion of its recitation is its own thing rather than a duplicate of the after-salah tasbih.</p>\n<h2 id=\"99-names\">The 99 Names of Allah</h2>\n<p>Asma ul-Husna is more than a counter that stops at ninety-nine. Each Name is shown with its Arabic, transliteration and a short meaning, and you can move through them one at a time, hide the meaning to test your memory, or select a single Name and count it with a target of your own choosing.</p>\n<p>No Abjad-derived repetition count is attached to any Name here. If you wish to set a number, that number is yours and the app makes no claim about it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tasbih-vs-misbaha\">Tasbih, tasbeeh, misbaha and dhikr: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The words overlap and the spellings vary by region, which is why a search for a tasbeeh counter and a search for a dhikr counter often mean the same thing.</p>\n<p>Tasbih usually means the act of glorifying Allah, and by extension the beads used to count it. Tasbeeh is the same word written a different way. Misbaha, sibha or sebha is the Arabic name for the bead string itself. Dhikr, also written zikr or zikir, is the wider act of remembrance, of which tasbih is one part. Tahlil means saying La ilaha illallah, tahmid means saying Alhamdulillah, takbir means saying Allahu Akbar and istighfar means seeking forgiveness.</p>\n<p>A physical misbaha has no batteries and needs no screen, and many people prefer it. A digital tasbih counter is easier to keep in a pocket, remembers your total between sittings and can hold several dhikr at once. Neither is more correct than the other.</p>\n<p>In other languages the same tool is called a tasbih counter in English, a تسبيح counter or مسبحة إلكترونية (an electronic misbaha) in Arabic, a تسبیح کاؤنٹر in Urdu, a तस्बीह काउंटर or digital तस्बीह in Hindi, a zikirmatik or dijital tesbih in Turkish, a penghitung tasbih or tasbih digital in Indonesian and Malay, and a তাসবিহ কাউন্টার in Bengali.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digital-tasbih\">Digital tasbih online: one counter for every screen</h2>\n<p>A digital tasbih is the simplest way to keep count of dhikr without carrying beads. This one runs in the browser on an Android phone, an iPhone, an iPad, a tablet, a laptop or a desktop computer, so the same online tasbih is with you at home, at work and in the masjid. There is no tasbih app to download and no update to wait for.</p>\n<p>It is built as a tap counter, so it also works as a tasbih clicker or a tally counter for anything you repeat. You tap the large surface, feel the tick and the vibration, and see the bead ring fill. If you searched for a digital tasbih for mobile, a tasbih counter for PC or a free online tasbeeh, this is the page you were looking for.</p>\n<h2 id=\"umrah-hajj\">Tawaf and Sa'i counter for Umrah and Hajj</h2>\n<p>Pilgrims also lose count. Tasbih Counts includes a Tawaf counter of seven rounds and a Sa'i counter of seven lengths between Safa and Marwa. Each tap records one round, the counter stops at seven so you cannot go past it, and the screen stays awake for the whole rite. Find both in the dhikr list.</p>\n<p>This is a simple aid to keep your place. Follow the guidance of your group leader or scholar for how to perform Umrah and Hajj.</p>\n<h2 id=\"languages\">Tasbih counter in your language</h2>\n<p>The page is written in English, and the Arabic of every dhikr is always shown as written. In Settings you can choose a language for how numbers and dates are shown and switch between Western numerals (123) and Eastern Arabic numerals (١٢٣). Languages available: English, العربية, اردو, Bahasa Indonesia, हिन्दी, Türkçe, বাংলা, Bahasa Melayu and Français.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-web\">Why use a web counter instead of a tasbih counter app?</h2>\n<p>Most tasbih counter apps ask for install permissions, carry advertising inside the counting screen and, according to their own data safety disclosures, may share location and app activity with third parties.</p>\n<p>This is a website. It opens in one tap from a link, it can be added to your home screen so it behaves like an app, and it never asks for your contacts, your location or an account. Nothing about what you recite is sent anywhere, because there is nowhere for it to go.</p>\n<p>There is one honest trade-off. A native app can count using the volume buttons and a website cannot. On a computer you can count with the space bar instead, and on a phone the tap surface fills most of the screen so you rarely need to look at it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"offline\">Does the tasbih counter work offline?</h2>\n<p>Yes. After your first visit the counter, the dhikr list, the guided routines, the 99 Names, your history and your streak are all stored on your device. Turn off mobile data and it still counts.</p>\n<p>Your count is saved as you go and it survives closing the tab, locking the phone and restarting the browser. Because everything lives on your device rather than on a server, clearing your browser data will clear it too, and you can download a backup file at any time from More. With Premium, your practice is also kept in your account.</p>\n<h2 id=\"wazifa\">Wazifa, Ramadan and long counts</h2>\n<p>If you keep a daily wazifa, set your own phrase and your own daily goal and the counter will track it without comment. A wazifa counter here is exactly that: a number you chose, tracked honestly. It does not label your number as a sunnah, because that would be a claim the app is in no position to make.</p>\n<p>The same applies to Ramadan dhikr, to a tahajjud sitting, and to the long goals people set themselves such as one lakh or one crore of a single phrase. Targets accept up to seven digits, the number is grouped with separators so a six figure count is still readable at a glance, and the round counter keeps the sitting from becoming one undifferentiated block.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tips\">Tips for daily dhikr</h2>\n<p>A small amount kept up consistently is worth more than a large amount abandoned after a week. Choose a target you can complete on a bad day, not on your best one.</p>\n<p>Attach the count to something you already do. The remembrance after salah is the natural anchor, which is why it is the most used routine here. If you count while walking or in the car, turn on vibration and stop looking at the screen; the haptic is designed to be felt rather than watched.</p>\n<p>If you lose your place, use undo rather than starting again. If you finish a target and want to keep going, choose Continue counting rather than Reset, and your session total stays intact.</p>\n<h2 id=\"faq\">Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is this online tasbih counter really free?</h3>\n<p>Yes. The counter, the dhikr library, guided routines, the 99 Names, your own dhikr, streaks, stats and backups are free, with no account. An optional one-time Premium adds sync across your devices, daily reminders and an ad-free site.</p>\n<h3>How many beads are in a tasbih?</h3>\n<p>Most tasbeeh strings have thirty-three beads, which are gone round three times to reach ninety-nine, or ninety-nine beads in a single loop. Some have one hundred. The bead ring on this page follows your chosen target rather than a fixed bead count.</p>\n<h3>Do I need to download an app?</h3>\n<p>No. This works in your browser on any device. If you want it on your home screen, use your browser's Add to Home Screen or Install option and it opens full screen like an app, still with no download from a store.</p>\n<h3>What is the 33 33 34 dhikr?</h3>\n<p>It is the remembrance said after the obligatory prayer: SubhanAllah thirty-three times, Alhamdulillah thirty-three times and Allahu Akbar thirty-four times, completing one hundred. A second transmitted form counts thirty-three of each and completes the hundred with La ilaha illallah. Both are available here as guided routines.</p>\n<h3>What is a digital tasbih?</h3>\n<p>A digital tasbih is a tasbih counter on a screen instead of a string of beads. You tap once for each repetition of your dhikr and the number goes up. This free online digital tasbih also keeps your total, your rounds and your streak.</p>\n<h3>Is there a tasbih counter for mobile and for PC?</h3>\n<p>Yes. It is one website that works on Android, iPhone, iPad, tablets, Windows, Mac and Linux, with a layout built for each. On a computer you can count with the space bar.</p>\n<h3>Is there a tally counter or tasbih clicker?</h3>\n<p>Yes. The whole counting area is one large tap target, so it behaves as a tasbih clicker or tally counter. It also has undo, a target, rounds, a countdown and auto count.</p>\n<h3>Is there a Tawaf counter or Sa'i counter for Umrah and Hajj?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Tawaf counts seven rounds and Sa'i counts seven lengths between Safa and Marwa. Both stop at seven and keep the screen awake.</p>\n<h3>Can I use this as a zikr counter or a dhikr counter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Tasbih counter, tasbeeh counter, dhikr counter and zikr counter all describe this tool. Choose any phrase from the list or add your own.</p>\n<h3>Is there a durood counter and a salawat counter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Salawat upon the Prophet is in the list, and it is found by searching durood, darood, darud, durood sharif or salat alan nabi. Many people keep it as a Friday count.</p>\n<h3>Is there an istighfar counter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Astaghfirullah and Astaghfirullaha wa atubu ilayh are both included, with one hundred offered as a source-backed daily target.</p>\n<h3>Is there a tahlil counter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. La ilaha illallah is in the list and is found by searching tahlil, tahleel or kalima.</p>\n<h3>Can I count the 99 Names of Allah?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Asma ul-Husna is included with all ninety-nine Names, each with Arabic, transliteration and meaning, and you can also count a single Name with your own target.</p>\n<h3>Is there a tasbih counter with sound and vibration?</h3>\n<p>Yes, and both are optional. Sound is a short synthesised tick with no file to download. Vibration works on Android and on browsers that support it; Safari on iPhone and iPad has no vibration support at all, so sound is the substitute there.</p>\n<h3>Can I count with the screen off, or in my pocket?</h3>\n<p>No, and no web counter can. A page in a browser stops receiving taps the moment the screen locks, and a website is not allowed to run in the background or read the volume buttons the way an installed app can. What this does instead is keep the screen awake while you count, and give you a tick and a haptic strong enough to feel with your eyes closed, so you can hold the phone loosely in your lap and never look at it. If counting with the screen off matters more to you than anything else, an installed app or a physical tasbih is the honest recommendation.</p>\n<h3>Can I count without looking at the screen?</h3>\n<p>Yes, and this is what the sound and vibration are for. Turn on the haptic, turn on Keep screen awake, and the whole counting area is one tap target, so your thumb does not have to find a button. On an iPhone or iPad there is no vibration available to any website, so turn the sound on there instead. Auto count also runs at your own learned pace so your hands are free entirely.</p>\n<h3>Does it have a tasbih counter with no ads?</h3>\n<p>Ads never appear inside the counting area. The free website carries a few ads around the article, and Premium — a single payment, for life — removes them from the whole site.</p>\n<h3>Can I set a custom target like 313 or 1000?</h3>\n<p>Yes, and any number up to seven digits. Custom numbers are shown as your own goal, never as a religious recommendation.</p>\n<h3>Does the counter save my count if I close the page?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Your count, target, rounds and history are saved on your device as you go, and each dhikr keeps its own count, so switching between SubhanAllah and Astaghfirullah does not wipe either one.</p>\n<h3>What is a digital misbaha?</h3>\n<p>Misbaha is the Arabic word for the prayer bead string. A digital misbaha is the same idea on a screen, which is exactly what this online tasbih counter is.</p>\n<h3>Is there a tasbih Fatima counter?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Tasbih Fatimah is offered as its own guided routine of thirty-three, thirty-three and thirty-four.</p>\n<h3>Can I use it in Ramadan?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Use it for daily dhikr, istighfar and salawat through the month. Full screen and dark mode help at night, and the streak and totals show your month at a glance.</p>\n<h3>Can I use it for a wazifa or a daily target?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Add your own phrase, set your own daily goal and count it. The app tracks the number you chose and makes no claim about what a particular number achieves.</p>\n<h3>Does it work on iPhone, Android, tablet and desktop?</h3>\n<p>Yes, from one address, with a layout built for each. On a phone the tap surface fills most of the screen; on a tablet and a desktop the tool stays in a comfortable centred column and you can count with the space bar.</p>\n<h3>Is my dhikr data private?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Without an account, what you recite and how often never leaves your device. If you buy Premium, your counts sync to your account so they follow you to every device you sign in on — and they are never sold or used for advertising. You can download a backup, or clear everything from this device, at any time from More.</p>\n<h3>The Arabic or the meaning looks wrong. What should I do?</h3>\n<p>Please tell us. Mistakes in the religious content are corrected quickly. Write to contact@tasbihcounts.com with the phrase and what is wrong with it.</p>";

export const aboutUs: string = "<h1>About Us</h1>\n<h2>Why Tasbih Counts Exists</h2>\n<p>Most dhikr counters make you work for the count. Some hide the tap target behind a small button, some put an advertisement in the middle of the tool, and several lose everything the moment you reload the page.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasbih Counts</strong> is built the other way round. The <strong><a href=\"/\">online tasbih counter</a></strong> is the first thing on the page, the whole counting area is the tap target, and nothing about pricing or signing in appears before you have counted.</p>\n<h2>How It Is Built</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No account to count.</strong> The counter, the dhikr library, guided routines, the 99 Names, your own dhikr, streaks and stats are free for everyone, with no sign-up.</li>\n<li><strong>Offline first.</strong> After the first visit it works with no connection at all, and it installs to your home screen.</li>\n<li><strong>Your dhikr stays yours.</strong> Without an account, nothing you count leaves your device. We never sell your data or use your practice for advertising.</li>\n<li><strong>Calm on purpose.</strong> No streak guilt, no confetti and no notifications chasing you back — unless you ask for a daily reminder.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The Religious Content</h2>\n<p>Every phrase, transliteration, meaning and count is kept under version control and reviewed. Nothing is machine translated, and no phrase is published on the strength of a search result.</p>\n<p>Counts are labelled honestly. A count backed by a source is shown as such; a number you chose for yourself is shown as your own goal and never dressed up as a recommendation. This is a counting tool, not a religious authority — if something here is wrong, we would rather hear it than have it stand.</p>\n<h2>Free, and What Is Paid</h2>\n<p>The counter and everything you need to remember Allah are free permanently. An optional one-time <a href=\"/premium/\">Premium</a> plan pays for the running costs: it removes advertising and adds sync across your devices and daily reminders. Convenience, in other words — never the ability to count your dhikr.</p>\n<h2>Contact</h2>\n<p>Questions, corrections to the Arabic or a meaning, billing and account deletion: <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong>. Every message is read by a person and answered.</p>";

export const contactUs: string = "<h1>Contact Us</h1>\n<h2><strong>Get In Touch</strong></h2>\n<p>We’d love to hear from you! For any questions, feedback, or support related to <strong>Tasbih Counts</strong>, reach out anytime.</p>\n<p><strong>Email:</strong> <a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></p>\n<p>We’re happy to help with technical issues, feature suggestions, Premium and payments, or guidance on using our <strong><a href=\"/\">online tasbih counter</a></strong>.<br>We reply within 24–48 hours.</p>\n<p>If the Arabic, a transliteration or a meaning looks wrong, please tell us the phrase and what you believe the correct form to be. Corrections are read by a person and fixed quickly.</p>\n<p>Your privacy matters — your details and your practice remain completely confidential.</p>";

export const privacyPolicy: string = "<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>\n<p><em>Last updated: 13 September 2026</em></p>\n<h2><strong>Our Commitment to Your Privacy</strong></h2>\n<p>Tasbih Counts is a digital tasbih counter. Your practice is personal, and this policy explains plainly what information is involved when you use the website, where it goes, and the choices you have. It covers both the free counter and the optional Premium plan.</p>\n<p>Tasbih Counts is run by an individual in India and is responsible for the personal data described here under India’s Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023.</p>\n<h3><strong>Using the Free Counter</strong></h3>\n<p>You do not need an account, and we never ask for your name, email or phone number.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Your practice stays on your device:</strong> counts, chosen dhikr, targets, timers, favourites, settings, streak and day-by-day history are saved in your browser’s local storage. They are not sent to us.</li>\n<li><strong>Backups are files you keep:</strong> Settings → Back up downloads a file to your device, and Restore reads a file you pick. We never receive these files.</li>\n<li><strong>Clearing is in your hands:</strong> clearing this website’s data in your browser removes everything the counter saved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Advertising</strong></h3>\n<p>The free website shows ads served by Google AdSense.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Third-party vendors, including Google, use cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website and other websites.</li>\n<li>Google’s use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to serve ads to you based on your visits to this website and/or other websites on the Internet.</li>\n<li>You can opt out of personalised advertising in <a href=\"https://adssettings.google.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Google Ads Settings</a>, and opt out of some other vendors’ use of cookies at <a href=\"https://www.aboutads.info/choices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">aboutads.info</a>.</li>\n<li>Google explains how it uses information from websites that use its services in <a href=\"https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">How Google uses information from sites or apps that use our services</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Premium users are not shown ads.</p>\n<h3><strong>Analytics</strong></h3>\n<p>We use Google Analytics to understand how the website is used in general — which pages are visited, and which devices and browsers are common — so we can fix problems and improve it. Google Analytics sets cookies and receives information such as the pages you view, your device and browser, and your approximate location. It never receives your counts, dhikr or history. You can block it with the <a href=\"https://tools.google.com/dlpage/gaoptout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Google Analytics opt-out add-on</a> or your browser’s cookie settings.</p>\n<h3><strong>Premium</strong></h3>\n<p>If you buy Premium, some information has to leave your device so we can take your payment, create your account and sync your practice. We collect only what that needs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Email address:</strong> given at checkout, and used to create your account, let you sign in, and send sign-in codes, payment confirmations and important notices about your account. We do not send marketing email.</li>\n<li><strong>Google sign-in:</strong> if you choose it, Google shares your name, email address and profile picture with us. We use the email to find your Premium account. We never get your Google password or anything else in your Google account.</li>\n<li><strong>Payment records:</strong> Razorpay handles the payment. We receive the payment and order IDs, the amount, date, status and payment method type (for example UPI or card) — never your card number, UPI PIN or bank login.</li>\n<li><strong>Your synced practice:</strong> counts per day and per dhikr, targets, settings and streak history, so the same practice appears on every device you sign in on. Each device gets an identifier so that counts from two devices add up correctly.</li>\n<li><strong>Reminders:</strong> if you turn them on, your browser’s push notification token and your time zone, so a reminder arrives at the right time.</li>\n<li><strong>Security records:</strong> basic technical details such as IP address and the time of sign-in and payment requests, to keep accounts safe and stop abuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Support messages:</strong> whatever you choose to tell us when you write to us.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We use this information only to provide Premium to you and to meet legal obligations, such as keeping payment records under Indian tax law. We do not sell or rent personal data, and we do not use it for advertising.</p>\n<h3><strong>Who Processes It for Us</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supabase:</strong> database and sign-in for Premium accounts.</li>\n<li><strong>Razorpay:</strong> payment processing.</li>\n<li><strong>Google:</strong> Google sign-in, Firebase Cloud Messaging for reminders, Google Analytics and Google AdSense.</li>\n<li><strong>Hostinger:</strong> hosting of the website.</li>\n<li><strong>An email delivery service:</strong> sending sign-in codes and account emails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These providers process data only to provide their service to us, and some of them store data on servers outside India. We share personal data with authorities only when Indian law requires it.</p>\n<h3><strong>How Long We Keep It</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Account and synced practice:</strong> while your account exists. After you delete your account, it is erased from our live database within 30 days, and from backups when those backups expire.</li>\n<li><strong>Payment records:</strong> for as long as Indian tax and accounting law requires, even after the account is deleted.</li>\n<li><strong>Push notification tokens:</strong> until you turn reminders off, sign out on that device, or delete your account.</li>\n<li><strong>Security records:</strong> up to 90 days, unless they are needed to investigate abuse.</li>\n<li><strong>Support emails:</strong> up to one year after the conversation ends.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Your Rights</strong></h3>\n<p>Under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ask what personal data we hold about you and how it is used;</li>\n<li>ask us to correct or update it;</li>\n<li>ask us to delete your account and its data;</li>\n<li>withdraw your consent — for Premium, that means deleting your account, because the service cannot run without it;</li>\n<li>nominate someone to exercise these rights for you in case of death or incapacity;</li>\n<li>raise a grievance with us and, if our answer does not resolve it, complain to the Data Protection Board of India.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Write to <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong> from the email address on your account. We usually reply within 24–48 hours, and always within 3 working days, and complete requests within 30 days. Deleting your account does not refund the Premium payment. The free counter’s data exists only in your browser, so you control it directly there.</p>\n<h3><strong>Security</strong></h3>\n<p>All connections to the website are encrypted. Premium data is stored with access rules that let each account read only its own data, and every payment is verified with Razorpay before Premium is activated. No system is perfectly secure; if a breach affects your personal data, we will inform you and the Data Protection Board of India as the law requires.</p>\n<h3><strong>Children</strong></h3>\n<p>The free counter collects no personal data from anyone, of any age. Premium is for adults: if you are under 18, a parent or guardian must buy it for you and agree to this policy on your behalf.</p>\n<h3><strong>Changes to This Policy</strong></h3>\n<p>When we change this policy we update the date at the top. If a change affects how Premium users’ data is used, we will email them before it takes effect.</p>\n<h3><strong>Contact and Grievances</strong></h3>\n<p>Privacy questions, requests and grievances: <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong> — please put “Privacy” or “Grievance” in the subject.</p>\n<h3><strong>Our Assurance</strong></h3>\n<p>Tasbih Counts was created to serve Muslims everywhere who want to keep up their dhikr.<br>Your remembrance is personal — we will never sell your data or use your practice for advertising.</p>";

export const terms: string = "<h1>Terms of Service</h1>\n<p><em>Last updated: 10 September 2026</em></p>\n<h2>About These Terms</h2>\n<p>These terms apply when you use Tasbih Counts (“Tasbih Counts”, “we”, “us”), the digital tasbih counter on this website, including the free counter and the optional Premium plan. By using the website you agree to them. If you do not agree, please do not use the website.</p>\n<p>Tasbih Counts is run by an individual in India. It is not a religious organisation, and nothing on the website is religious, medical or professional advice.</p>\n<h3><strong>The Free Counter</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The counter is free to use and needs no account.</li>\n<li>Your counts, dhikr, targets, settings, streak and history are saved in your own browser on your own device. We do not receive them.</li>\n<li>Because that data lives in your browser, clearing site data, private browsing, or changing device or browser can remove it. <strong>Settings → Back up</strong> saves a copy to your device and <strong>Restore</strong> brings it back. Keeping your backup files safe is your responsibility.</li>\n<li>The free website shows advertising.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Premium</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Premium is a one-time payment of ₹200 for lifetime access. There is no subscription, no renewal and no free trial.</li>\n<li>Premium removes advertising and adds sign-in, cloud sync of your counter data across your devices, and optional reminders. The features included are the ones described on the website at the time you buy.</li>\n<li>An account is created only after a successful Premium payment, using the email address given at checkout. There is no other way to register.</li>\n<li>“Lifetime” means for as long as Tasbih Counts operates the Premium plan. If we ever close it, we will email Premium users at least 30 days before, and you will be able to download your data.</li>\n<li>You must be 18 or older to buy Premium, or have a parent or guardian buy it for you.</li>\n<li>A Premium account is for one person. Please do not share it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Payments</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Payments are processed by Razorpay. We never see or store your card, UPI or bank details.</li>\n<li>The price shown at checkout, in Indian Rupees, is the amount you pay.</li>\n<li>Refunds are covered by our <a href=\"/refund-policy/\">Refund &amp; Cancellation Policy</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Your Account</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep your email and Google accounts secure: anyone who can sign in to them can sign in to your Tasbih Counts account.</li>\n<li>You can ask us to delete your account at any time. Deletion is permanent and does not refund the Premium payment.</li>\n<li>We may suspend or close an account that is used to attack, abuse or overload the service, or that was paid for fraudulently.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Acceptable Use</strong></h3>\n<p>Please do not try to break, probe or overload the website or its servers, access anyone else’s data, copy or resell the website or its content, or use it for anything unlawful.</p>\n<h3><strong>Content and Ownership</strong></h3>\n<p>The website’s design, code, text, logo and artwork belong to Tasbih Counts. The words of dhikr belong to no one; you are free to recite them anywhere. You may share links to the website freely.</p>\n<h3><strong>Availability and Changes</strong></h3>\n<p>We work to keep the website running and your data safe, but we cannot promise it will always be available or free of errors. We may add, change or remove features. If a change takes away something Premium includes, we will tell Premium users by email.</p>\n<h3><strong>Disclaimer</strong></h3>\n<p>Tasbih Counts is a tool to support your practice and is provided “as is”. The benefits of dhikr are personal to you and are not promised by us.</p>\n<h3><strong>Limitation of Liability</strong></h3>\n<p>To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, including the loss of counts or history kept only in your browser. Our total liability to you for anything relating to the website is limited to the amount you paid us for Premium.</p>\n<h3><strong>Governing Law</strong></h3>\n<p>These terms are governed by the laws of India, and the courts of India have jurisdiction over any dispute.</p>\n<h3><strong>Changes to These Terms</strong></h3>\n<p>We may update these terms; the date at the top shows the current version. We will email Premium users about significant changes before they take effect. Using the website after a change means you accept the updated terms.</p>\n<h3><strong>Contact</strong></h3>\n<p>Questions about these terms: <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong></p>";

export const refundPolicy: string = "<h1>Refund &amp; Cancellation Policy</h1>\n<p><em>Last updated: 13 September 2026</em></p>\n<h2>What You Are Buying</h2>\n<p>Tasbih Counts Premium is a one-time payment of ₹200 for lifetime access. It is a digital service: nothing is shipped, and there is no free trial, so please read what Premium includes before you pay.</p>\n<h3><strong>Delivery</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Premium is activated as soon as Razorpay confirms your payment, usually within a minute.</li>\n<li>Your account is created with the email address you gave at checkout, and you can sign in with it straight away.</li>\n<li>If Premium is not active 30 minutes after a successful payment, write to us with your payment ID and we will activate it or refund you in full.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Cancellation</strong></h3>\n<p>Premium is not a subscription. Nothing renews, so there is nothing to cancel and you will never be charged again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If you close the checkout before paying, no money is taken.</li>\n<li>A completed payment cannot be cancelled.</li>\n<li>You can stop using Premium or ask us to delete your account at any time, but that does not make the payment refundable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>When You Get a Refund</strong></h3>\n<p>Refunds are given only for a technical payment problem:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>money was taken but Premium was never activated, and we cannot activate it for you;</li>\n<li>you were charged more than once for the same purchase — every extra charge is refunded;</li>\n<li>you were charged more than the price shown at checkout — the difference is refunded.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every request is checked against Razorpay’s payment records.</p>\n<h3><strong>When a Refund Is Not Given</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>You changed your mind after Premium was activated.</li>\n<li>You did not use Premium, or used it less than you expected.</li>\n<li>Your account was closed for breaking our <a href=\"/terms/\">Terms of Service</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>Failed Payments</strong></h3>\n<p>If your bank shows a debit but the payment failed, the amount is returned to you automatically by your bank, usually within 5–7 working days. You do not need to ask us, but we are glad to help you check.</p>\n<h3><strong>How to Ask for a Refund</strong></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Email <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong> within 30 days of the payment, with “Refund” in the subject. Many people only notice a double charge on a monthly statement, so there is time for that.</li>\n<li>Include the email used at checkout, the date and amount of the payment, and the Razorpay payment ID (it starts with “pay_” and is in Razorpay’s payment confirmation).</li>\n<li>We usually reply within 24–48 hours, and always within 3 working days.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3><strong>How Refunds Are Paid</strong></h3>\n<p>An approved refund goes back to the original payment method through Razorpay, for the full affected amount with nothing deducted. Your bank or card issuer usually shows it within 5–7 working days of approval. A payment that is refunded because Premium never activated does not give Premium access.</p>\n<h3><strong>Contact</strong></h3>\n<p>Refund and payment questions: <strong><a href=\"mailto:{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}\">{{SUPPORT_EMAIL}}</a></strong></p>";
